title: Commands
tags: GUI
kind: overview
aliases: command registry dispatch invocation
summary: Everything the GUI does is a named command — typed in the command input, bound to a key, clicked in a menu or sent by a script — and a command asks for the arguments it was not given.

A ''command'' is a named operation, with arguments: `panel:split`,
`workspace:goto 2`, `metarecord:field set rating 5`. Everything the GUI does is
one. The same invocation string runs it from anywhere:

* typed in [[the command input|The command input]];
* bound to a key ([[Keybindings]] map a key to an invocation, arguments included);
* clicked — a button or a menu entry invokes the command, it does not have code of its own ([[Context menus]]);
* sent by a script, with `mf gui command`.

The [[GUI command]] catalog has a note for each.

!! An invocation

A name, then arguments separated by spaces. Double quotes keep the spaces of an
argument (`metarecord-list:insert finder "two words" stay`), and the
''last'' argument a command declares takes everything that is left, so it needs no
quotes: `workspace:rename My music`.

An invocation starting with `!` is not a command but a shell line ([[Bash mode]]).

An argument left out is not an error: the command input asks for it
([[Interactive command arguments]]). So one learns a command by running it bare.

!! Where commands come from

* The ''shell'' defines the builtins: workspaces, panel slots, the command input, find, help, undo…
* Each ''panel type'' registers its own when it is built, under its name: `metarecord-list:next`, `file:zoom`.
* The user's `gui/commands.js` adds more ([[User commands]]).

The GUI builds every installed panel type once at startup, hidden, so that all the
commands exist from the start ([[Panel types]]). The names follow one rule
([[Command naming]]).

!! A command runs on its panel, focused or not

Every command is listed and can be run whatever panel has the focus. A panel's
command is handed to the panel type that registered it, in the ''focused workspace'':
acting on a panel that is not focused — or not even displayed — is legitimate. The
arrow keys of the `file` panel run `metarecord-list:next` and `metarecord-list:prev`
on the list of the other slot; a workspace that never showed the panel has it built
for the occasion.

A command registered with `reveal` shows its panel first when it is not on screen —
in the other slot when that one shows the same workspace, else in place of the
focused panel.

What depends on the focus is the ''key'', not the command: a keybinding may be
restricted to a panel type (`when`, [[Keybindings]]).

!! Every control is a command

Each button of a panel or of the shell runs a command, and each text field has a
command that focuses it (`command-input:focus command`, `metarecord-list:focus finder`,
`workspace:rename`). So everything can be bound to a key and driven by a script.
The controls ''inside'' a form a command opened — its Cancel, its submit — and those
tied to a position — one particular tab, one slot's `×` — have no command of their own.

!! What is written to the message log

Each invocation is written to the workspace's messages as `> name arguments`, before
it runs — with the arguments that were asked for, so the line is what actually ran.
A command may opt out (`log: false`): the keystroke-level ones do — `editing:*`,
`command-input:focus`, `completion:cycle`, `find:*`, `help*`, `status:clear`.

A command that fails says why in the status bar. An unknown name is ''unknown
command: …''.

!! Keys bound to nothing

A key bound to a command that does not exist runs nothing. Once every panel is built,
the GUI lists these bindings in the message log, key and invocation — a typo in
`keybindings.toml` ([[Changing keybindings]]), or a command renamed since. Only the first word of the invocation
is looked up, since the rest are arguments. A test checks the shipped keybindings the
same way.

